## What is Erlang?

_Erlang_ is a functional programming language. This means it is based on the principles of functions with referential transparency and immutability. Referential transparency means that a function with the same parameters should return the same result.  Immutability means that I cannot alter the value of a variable once it has been assigned; for example, if I say that x is 5, it is not logical for it to also be 6 (that would be dishonest). However, there are cases where a function can return a different result with the same parameters (for example a function that returns the current date). _Erlang_ takes a pragmatic approach: Obey the principles of functional programming (immutability, referential transparency) and break them when real-world conflicts appear.

In addition to the programming language, _Erlang_ has a whole ecosystem of tools. First there is its virtual machine (_BEAM VM_), which executes code compiled into a specific _bytecode_, very similar to _Java_'s _JVM_. So the code can be executed on any system for which the _BEAM VM_ is compatible. It also provides development tools such as compilers, debuggers, performance analysis and testing tools. The OTP framework, web servers, source code parsers, a distributed database called _mnesia_, among other tools. The virtual machine and its libraries allow updating code on the fly (hot reload), which means changing the code during program execution without service interruption, and also allow the code to run in a distributed fashion across several computers, handling errors and failures in a simple and powerful way.

All these characteristics give _Erlang_ an ability for resilience against errors and for organizing code into concurrent processes and messages.

## The Origin of Erlang

_Erlang_ began as a _Prolog_ library, then as a _Prolog_ dialect, to finally become a programming language in its own right. The goal was to solve the problem of building robust and reliable distributed systems. Originally developed at the telecommunications company _Ericsson_ for its telephone switches. From the beginning it faced worldwide-scale challenges such as hundreds of millions of users and very strict service conditions. It was released to the public in 1998 as an _Open Source_ project. Today, according to [Cisco](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=17218190), 90% of internet traffic is orchestrated by nodes programmed in _Erlang_.

_Erlang_ and the _BEAM VM_ have evolved over more than 30 years toward other use cases such as robotics, machine learning, web applications, among others. One of its main creators, _Joe Armstrong_, describes it in the [following way](https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/1238844.1238850):

Erlang was designed to write concurrent programs that run forever. Erlang uses concurrent processes to structure the program. These processes have no shared memory and communicate by asynchronous message passing. Erlang processes are lightweight and belong to the language, not the operating system. Erlang has mechanisms that allow programs to change on-the-fly (live), so those programs can evolve and change without stopping their execution. These mechanisms simplify the construction of software by implementing non-stop systems (that never halt).

The initial development of Erlang took place in 1986 at Ericsson's Computer Science Laboratory. Erlang was designed with one specific goal in mind: to provide a better way to program telecommunications applications. At the time, telecom applications were atypical of the kind of problems that conventional programming languages could solve. Telecom applications are, by their nature, highly concurrent: a simple switch must handle tens or hundreds of thousands of simultaneous transactions. Such transactions are intrinsically distributed and the software is expected to be highly fault tolerant. When the software that controls the phones fails, it makes the newspapers, something that does not happen when desktop applications fail. Telecom software must also change on-the-fly, that is, without losing service while a code update is performed. Telecom software must also operate in real time, with tight time requirements for some operations and more relaxed timing for other classes of operations.

